[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Backtracking: Exploring All Possible Solutions"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Backtracking is a powerful algorithmic technique used to solve problems by incrementally building candidate solutions and abandoning a candidate ("backtracking") as soon as it determines that the candidate cannot possibly lead to a valid solution. It's a refined brute-force approach that systematically searches for a solution among all possible options."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Core Concepts"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"<b>State Space Tree:</b> Backtracking can be visualized as exploring a tree where each node represents a partial solution. The root represents the initial state, and each level represents a decision point."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"<b>Decision Points:</b> At each node, we make a choice from a set of possible options. Each choice leads to a new node (a new partial solution)."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"<b>Constraints:</b> These are conditions that must be satisfied for a solution to be valid. If a partial solution violates a constraint, we backtrack."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"<b>Backtracking:</b> If a partial solution cannot be extended to a valid solution (either it violates a constraint or all options have been exhausted), we backtrack to the previous decision point and try a different option."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"<b>Goal State:</b> A complete and valid solution to the problem."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Algorithm Template"},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"function backtrack(partial_solution):
  if is_solution(partial_solution):
    return partial_solution

  for choice in possible_choices:
    new_partial_solution = extend_solution(partial_solution, choice)
    if is_valid(new_partial_solution):
      result = backtrack(new_partial_solution)
      if result:
        return result

    # Backtrack: Undo the choice
    remove_choice(partial_solution, choice)

  return null # No solution found from this point"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: N-Queens Problem"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"The N-Queens problem asks you to place N chess queens on an N×N chessboard so that no two queens threaten each other. Thus, a solution requires that no two queens share the same row, column, or diagonal."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"function solveNQueens(n):
  board = [['.' for _ in range(n)] for _ in range(n)]
  solutions = []

  def is_safe(row, col):
    # Check column
    for i in range(row):
      if board[i][col] == 'Q':
        return False

    # Check diagonals
    for i, j in zip(range(row - 1, -1, -1), range(col - 1, -1, -1)):
      if board[i][j] == 'Q':
        return False

    for i, j in zip(range(row - 1, -1, -1), range(col + 1, n)):
      if board[i][j] == 'Q':
        return False

    return True

  def backtrack(row):
    if row == n:
      solutions.append([''.join(row) for row in board])
      return

    for col in range(n):
      if is_safe(row, col):
        board[row][col] = 'Q'
        backtrack(row + 1)
        board[row][col] = '.' # Backtrack

  backtrack(0)
  return solutions"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"When to Use Backtracking"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"<b>Optimization Problems:</b> Finding the best solution among many possibilities (e.g., finding the shortest path)."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"<b>Constraint Satisfaction Problems:</b> Finding a solution that satisfies a set of constraints (e.g., Sudoku, N-Queens)."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"<b>Decision Problems:</b> Determining whether a solution exists (e.g., can a graph be colored with a certain number of colors?)."},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Advantages and Disadvantages"},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"<b>Advantages:</b> Can find all possible solutions or the optimal solution. Relatively easy to implement for some problems."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"<b>Disadvantages:</b> Can be very time-consuming, especially for large problem instances. May require significant memory to store the state space tree."},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"Backtracking is a valuable tool in your algorithmic arsenal. Understanding its principles and applying it judiciously can help you solve a wide range of complex problems."}]
